# Line endings, pinned explicitly.
#
# This file exists because of one concrete failure, not for tidiness.
#
# `deploy/entrypoint.sh` is the entrypoint of the Docker image. It starts with
# `#!/bin/sh`. If Git checks it out with CRLF endings, the kernel reads the
# interpreter line as `/bin/sh\r` and fails with:
#
#     /bin/sh^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#
# That is not a warning and not a slow degradation. The container does not start,
# and the error message says nothing about line endings. The same applies to
# `pbrforge.service` (systemd parses it strictly) and to `Dockerfile` (`RUN`
# continuations break). Windows' default `core.autocrlf=true` would do exactly
# this on any machine that clones the repo before building the image, which is
# every machine, because the build machine is Windows.
#
# The reverse also matters: `allow-firewall.cmd` has `goto` labels and a
# sub-elevation call. cmd.exe is documented-flaky about LF-only batch files
# around labels, so a batch file with LF endings can jump to the wrong line.
# In a script whose job is to add firewall rules, that is not a cosmetic issue.
#
# So: everything is LF by default, and the Windows-only script types are CRLF.

# Default: normalise to LF in the repository and on checkout.
* text=auto eol=lf

# --- CRLF: consumed by Windows tools that expect it -------------------------
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.psm1  text eol=crlf
*.psd1  text eol=crlf

# --- LF: consumed by Unix tools that require it ----------------------------
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
*.rules   text eol=lf
Dockerfile       text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile     text eol=lf
*.yml            text eol=lf
*.yaml           text eol=lf
*.toml           text eol=lf
*.json           text eol=lf
*.py             text eol=lf
*.md             text eol=lf
*.txt            text eol=lf
*.iss            text eol=lf
*.spec           text eol=lf
*.html           text eol=lf
.gitignore       text eol=lf
.gitattributes   text eol=lf

# --- Binary: never touch, never diff --------------------------------------
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.webp  binary
*.tif   binary
*.tiff  binary
*.exr   binary
*.hdr   binary
*.ico   binary
*.ttf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary

# --- Build output and binaries: no diffs, no filters ----------------------
*.exe   binary
*.dll   binary
*.pyd   binary
*.zip   binary
*.7z    binary
*.cubin binary

# --- Generated material maps: text detection on these is always wrong and
#     wastes time on every `git status` in a library folder ------------------
*_albedo_*.png   binary
*_normal_*.png   binary
*_roughness_*.png binary
*_metallic_*.png binary
*_height_*.png   binary

# --- Linguist: keep the GitHub language bar honest ------------------------
# The single-file UI is most of the line count; without this the repo reads as
# an HTML project rather than a Python one.
ui/prototype.html linguist-vendored=false linguist-documentation=false
docs/*.md         linguist-documentation
deploy/*.ps1      linguist-language=PowerShell
